{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>YHWH will fight for you.</b> Meaning — since the terror that fell upon them was from the angel, as we explained in the verse ״וּפַרְעֹה הִקְרִיב,״ therefore he said to them: It is well if they themselves were the combatants — they would reckon that he is stronger than they. But since YHWH is the one fighting, there is no need to worry about a thousand like him." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to say that even the attribute of mercy will turn into judgment against the Egyptians to fight against them, and it specified <b>for you</b> — since in relation to Israel the matter is accounted as mercy. Further, <b>He will fight for you</b> intends: that He will not be to them merely as help in battle, but rather He Himself will conduct the entire battle." "And the verse's saying <b>and you shall be silent</b> may be explained according to what they said, of blessed memory (Eichah Rabbati, Petichta 30): four righteous men — what this one asked, that one did not ask, and the choicest distinction among all of them is what Hezekiah asked, for he said: I have no strength — neither to kill, nor to pursue, nor to pray, but rather I lie upon my bed and You act. And so HaShem did, as it is written (II Kings 19:35): ״בַּלַּיְלָה הַהוּא וַיֵּצֵא מַלְאַךְ ה׳״ etc. And this is the station that Moses expressed: ״וְאַתֶּם תַּחֲרִישׁוּן״ — meaning, even in prayer, if you have no strength, as Hezekiah said — you shall be silent, and He will fight for you. And according to what we explained in the verse ״הִתְיַצְּבוּ״ — that he told them to stand in prayer — <b>and you shall be silent</b> intends along this path: to the attribute of judgment. Meaning, multiply in prayer to make your voice heard on high, so as to silence the attribute of judgment upon you."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:27:54.662644909Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}